{
  "response": "# Verdict: REJECT\n\n**Overall Assessment**: The component fails fundamentally on the most basic requirements of accessible form design. It lacks a programmatic label and fails to communicate error states to assistive technology, rendering the input unusable for screen reader users.\n\n**Pre-commitment Predictions**: \n- **Predicted**: Missing `<label>` element and programmatic association. (**Confirmed**)\n- **Predicted**: Error messages not associated via `aria-describedby`. (**Confirmed**)\n- **Predicted**: Missing `aria-invalid` state. (**Confirmed**)\n- **Predicted**: Help text for file restrictions missing for AT. (**Confirmed**)\n\n---\n\n## Findings\n\n### Critical Findings (Blocks Access)\n\n1. **Missing Accessible Name / Label Association**\n   - **Evidence**: `BuggyFileInput.jsx:22` \u2014 `<div>Upload file</div>`\n   - **User Group**: Screen reader users.\n   - **WCAG Citation**: 1.3.1 Info and Relationships, 4.1.2 Name, Role, Value.\n   - **Why this matters**: A `div` is not a label. When a screen reader user focuses the input, the AT will announce \"File upload, button\" (or similar) but will not announce \"Upload file.\" The user has no programmatic way to know what this input is for.\n   - **Fix**: Replace the `div` with a `<label>` element and associate it with the input using a unique `id` and the `htmlFor` attribute.\n\n2. **Error Messages Not Programmatically Associated**\n   - **Evidence**: `BuggyFileInput.jsx:27-31` \u2014 `{error && (<div className=\"error-message\">{error}</div>)}`\n   - **User Group**: Screen reader users.\n   - **WCAG Citation**: 1.3.1 Info and Relationships, 3.3.1 Error Identification.\n   - **Why this matters**: The error message is visually rendered but exists as a disconnected sibling in the DOM. A screen reader user focusing the input will not be notified that an error exists or what the error is. They must manually hunt through the DOM to find the error text.\n   - **Fix**: Give the error `div` a unique `id` and add `aria-describedby` to the `<input>` that references that ID when an error is present.\n\n### Major Findings (Significantly Degrades Experience)\n\n1. **Missing `aria-invalid` State Communication**\n   - **Evidence**: `BuggyFileInput.jsx:23` \u2014 `<input type=\"file\" ... />`\n   - **User Group**: Screen reader users.\n   - **WCAG Citation**: 4.1.2 Name, Role, Value.\n   - **Why this matters**: Even if the error text is associated, the input itself does not signal its \"invalid\" state. `aria-invalid=\"true\"` tells the user immediately upon focus that the current value is incorrect.\n   - **Fix**: Add `aria-invalid={!!error}` to the input element.\n\n### Enhancements (Best Practice)\n\n1. **Missing Programmatic Help Text (File Restrictions)**\n   - **Evidence**: `BuggyFileInput.jsx:25` \u2014 `accept=\".pdf,.doc,.docx\"`\n   - **User Group**: Screen reader and cognitive users.\n   - **WCAG Citation**: 3.3.2 Labels or Instructions.\n   - **Why this matters**: The `accept` attribute restricts the file picker dialog, but it does not provide a text instruction to the user *before* they click. Users should know the required formats upfront.\n   - **Fix**: Add a visible help text string (e.g., \"Accepted formats: PDF, DOC, DOCX\") and associate it with the input via `aria-describedby`.\n\n---\n\n## What's Missing\n\n- **Programmatic Label**: No `<label>` or `aria-label`.\n- **State Sync**: No `aria-invalid` to signal error state.\n- **Relationship Mapping**: No `aria-describedby` to link the input to its error or help text.\n- **Cognitive Guidance**: The error \"File too large\" is descriptive but not prescriptive. It should suggest a fix (e.g., \"File too large. Please upload a file smaller than 5MB\").\n\n## Multi-Perspective Notes\n\n| Perspective | Alarm Level | Trigger Signal |\n|-------------|-------------|----------------|\n| Screen reader | **HIGH** | No label, no error association, no state communication. |\n| Keyboard-only | **LOW** | Tab order is native and functional. |\n| Low vision | **MEDIUM** | Error is visual-only; depends on CSS contrast (not provided). |\n| Cognitive | **MEDIUM** | Error message lacks a clear path to resolution. |\n| Vestibular | **LOW** | No motion present. |\n| Auditory | **LOW** | No audio present. |\n| Env. Contrast | **MEDIUM** | Error text color needs verification against background. |\n\n- **Screen reader user**: Experience is broken. They encounter an unlabeled input and are not notified of errors unless they happen to navigate to the error `div` by chance.\n- **Keyboard-only user**: Can navigate and trigger the file picker, but lacks the context provided by the visual label.\n- **Cognitive user**: May be confused by the \"File too large\" message if they don't know the specific limit.\n\n## Verdict Justification\n\nThe component is **REJECTED** because it violates the most fundamental tenets of accessible form design. The lack of a programmatic label (`<label>`) and the failure to associate error messages (`aria-describedby`) create a complete barrier for screen reader users. These are not \"polish\" issues; they are critical accessibility failures.\n\n**To move to ACCEPT, the following must be implemented:**\n1. A proper `<label>` with `htmlFor`.\n2. `aria-invalid` toggled based on the error state.\n3. `aria-describedby` linking the input to the error message.\n4. Visible and programmatic instructions regarding allowed file types.",
